== DateFormat ==
A good way to format Date class, you can use Calendar.getTime() or the class Date
Based on your JVM localization

-> To format to a specific country and language, you can use the class Locale(language, country) inside the DateFormat constructor
(LocaleTest01)

== NumberFormat ==
A good way to format numbers, NumberFormat.getInstance(Locale) 10,000.219 -> 10.000,219
-> To format different currencies, use NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance(Locale)
-> You can parse String values to number values of determinate localization or currency of determinate localization to number values

== SimpleDateFormat ==
A custom pattern to format class Date -> "'São Paulo', dd 'de' MMMM 'de' yyyy, H 'horas' 'e' mm 'minutos'"

== DateTimeFormatter ==
+ You can create your own formatters like: "DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("pattern")" or use "DateTimeFormatter.ENUM"

